"Run across the playground as fast as you can, and then stop. Your heart is pounding, you are breathing hard, and your face is hot. Nothing is wrong. Your body has just done something clever: several of its systems have sped up together to help your muscles. It starts in the muscles. A working muscle uses energy many times faster than a resting one, and to get that energy it needs oxygen and fuel. Both arrive in the blood. So when your leg muscles start to work hard, they send out a call for more blood. Your lungs and your heart answer the call. You breathe faster and deeper, so more oxygen enters your blood. Your heart beats faster, and each beat pushes out more blood. At rest, the heart of a child about ten years old beats about 60 to 100 times a minute. In a hard run it can beat much faster than that. At the same time, your blood vessels send more of the blood to the muscles that are working and less to places that can wait, such as your stomach. All that work makes heat. To keep from getting too hot, your body sends warm blood to the skin, which is why your face turns red, and it makes sweat. As you learned in the lesson about skin, sweat cools you when it dries. That is also why you need to drink water when you exercise. When you stop, your heartbeat and breathing do not drop at once. For a few minutes your body keeps working to pay back the oxygen it borrowed and to cool down. The better your fitness, the faster you recover. And fitness can be trained. If you are active often, your heart muscle grows stronger and pushes out more blood with every beat, so it does not have to beat as often. Some top long-distance athletes have a resting heart rate below 50. How much is enough? The World Health Organization says that children and teenagers should be active for an average of at least 60 minutes a day, hard enough to make them breathe faster. It does not have to be a sport: fast walking, dancing, cycling, and playing tag all count. In Taiwan, schools also plan at least 150 minutes of activity a week outside of PE class. Now find out what your own heart does: use the pulse card below the model.": "c5f511a39b03",
